-- Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One, four robots (printed 103-116): Beachmaster, Behemoth Explorer, Behemoth Super-Explorer, Bigfoot
-- 4 vehicle rows, with 54 locations and 15 weapon entries.
--
-- One-off data script, run once per environment. NOT a migration - it adds
-- rows, not schema.
--
--   node scripts/d1-apply.mjs --local apps/character-creator/db/~285-northern-gun-1-robots-a.sql
--
-- Written by scripts/vessel-sql.mjs from 1 worker file(s). Every value
-- is printable ASCII, every row cites its book, and a Main Body location
-- agrees with its vehicle's mdc_main_body.

INSERT OR IGNORE INTO vehicles (slug, name, system, vehicle_class, crew, passengers, speed_ground, speed_air, speed_water, dimensions, weight_tons, mdc_main_body, is_mega_damage, ar, cost, cost_note, description, source_book) VALUES
  ('ng-v36-beachmaster', 'Beachmaster Amphibious Combat Robot', 'rifts', 'robot', '2: a pilot and a copilot/gunner; one pilot can run it alone if need be', NULL, 'Running 60 mph (96 km) maximum; leaps 10 ft (3 m) high or across, +50% with a running start', 'Not applicable', 'Up to 30 mph (48 km) on the surface; about 20 mph (32 km) submerged and 20 mph (32 km) walking the lake floor; maximum depth 4,000 ft (1,219 m)', 'Height 26 ft (8 m); width 16 ft (4.8 m) shoulder to shoulder; length 15 ft (4.6 m)', '25', 540, 1, NULL, 20000000, '28 million credits nuclear, 23 million Solid Oxide Fuel Cell, 20 million electric battery; the lowest is stored.', 'Model Type: NG-V36. Class: Amphibious Assault Robot (headed Beachmaster Amphibious Assault Robot on p.103; the name here is the book''s Alphabetical List of NG Combat Robots, p.100). The big combat-robot partner of NG''s Beach-Stormer power armor: it waits on lake or sea floors, uses a ballast system to rise under passing ships, climbs aboard or clings to a hull, and on land leads amphibious troops against bunkers, gun nests and wire. Its big hands also dig, climb and push up earth ramps for vehicles coming ashore. Robot P.S. 40. Cargo: a small locker beside the pilot (a rifle and survival kit) and a back pod holding an inflatable life raft plus up to 150 lbs (68 kg). Power: nuclear, 20 year average life; fuel cell and battery models are offered too. Hand to hand per Heavy Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352). Sensors: sonar and radar, telescopic, passive nightvision and polarized optics, plus every standard NG robot feature. Weapon 7 is the optional Ship Hunter variant''s rail cannon, fitted instead of the mini-torpedo launcher.', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.103-106'),
  ('ng-ex5-behemoth-explorer', 'Behemoth Explorer', 'rifts', 'robot', 'Three: pilot, copilot and communications officer', '20 to 40 comfortably; 50-60 crowded', 'Running 40 mph (64 km) maximum. The block says leaping is not possible, then gives a leap of 10 ft (3 m) high and long that costs two melee actions and lands with a loud crash', 'Not possible', 'Cannot swim; walks the lake floor at 12 mph (19.2 km); maximum depth 1,100 ft (335 m)', 'Height 60 ft (18.2 m); width 20 ft (6.1 m); length 82 ft (25 m)', '25', 500, 1, NULL, 85000000, 'First sold at 100 million credits new, now 85 million. Used and rebuilt, missing 3D6% of its M.D.C.: 40-60 million. Battered, at half M.D.C. and sensors: 20-30 million. Fair availability. No electric battery version.', 'Model Type: EX-5 Explorer (headed EX-5 Behemoth Explorer). Class: Behemoth Class Mobile Medical and Research Robot Vehicle. One of Northern Gun''s oldest designs, built every year for 22 years: a two-legged walking house trailer and field laboratory. Inside are ten bunk rooms of 2-4 berths with two small lavatories, heating and air conditioning; a dining area and observation deck in the glass dome on top (20 M.D.C. per 12 ft/3.6 m of plexiglass); a bay for four small vehicles and about six suits of power armor; and a small med-lab that doubles as sick bay. Cargo: 15x40x12 ft (4.6x12.2x3.6 m), 200 ton limit. The crew sits in the nose between the two big searchlights. Big grasping arms, with a smaller pair of retractable arms and hands inside the forearms. Robot P.S. 38 for the large arms, 21 for the small. Power: nuclear only, 12 year average life. Widely treated as a non-combatant, so bandits, Simvan and Xiticix usually let it pass, a courtesy lost once owners bolt extra guns on. Hand to hand at half the attacks and half the Heavy Ground Robot Training bonuses (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352): large arms 3D6 M.D. punch, tear or pry, 2D6 M.D. crush, power punch 5D6+3 M.D.; retractable arms 1D4 M.D., no power punch; stomp or trample 2D6 M.D.; body block 2D6 M.D. with a 01-70% chance to knock down a foe of its size or smaller. Sensors beyond the NG standard: thermal imager (2,000 ft/610 m), infrared and ultraviolet optics, infrared searchlights in the front headlights (500 ft/152 m), enhanced radar tracking 96 targets at 100 miles (160 km), and four telescopic and macro cameras (4,000 ft/1,219 m).', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.106-107'),
  ('ng-ex9-behemoth-super-explorer', 'Behemoth Super-Explorer', 'rifts', 'robot', 'Four: pilot, copilot, communications officer and a dedicated gunner; one pilot can drive it if necessary', '40 passengers/research team', 'Running 45 mph (72 km) maximum; leaping not possible; can limp on two of its four legs at one third speed', 'Not possible', 'Cannot swim; walks the lake floor at 12 mph (19.2 km); maximum depth 1,500 ft (457 m)', 'Height 70 ft (21.3 m); width 40 ft (12.2 m); length 110 ft (33.5 m)', '35', 700, 1, NULL, 142000000, 'Black Market Cost: 142 million credits new; too new for any used ones to be on the market. Fair availability. Short-range missile launchers in place of the mini-missile ones add 500,000 credits, medium-range add one million.', 'Model Type: EX-9 Super-Explorer (the printed line repeats Model Type). Class: Behemoth Class Mobile Medical & Research Robot Vehicle. Nickname: Super-X. A larger, four-legged successor to the EX-5, newly on the open market and meant to keep the EX-5''s non-combatant standing. Top level: two redundant sensor arrays, an enclosed observation deck under 8 inch (20.3 cm) plexiglass with armored shutters (30 M.D.C. per 12 ft/3.6 m section) holding a kitchen for 120, dining for 60, lounge, meeting room, library, small lab, greenhouse and storeroom; a sealable forward observation deck (130 M.D.C., hatch 120) seating six; and two rooftop hangar airlocks (200 M.D.C. each) for power armor or rocket bikes. Second level: main laboratory, a medical bay at small-clinic level with a full kit of robot medical systems, two 30-berth barracks, two crew rooms, the pilot''s room, storage, and an Auxiliary Bridge Control that can drive the robot by camera (hatch 120 M.D.C., wall 100 per 12 ft section). Interior doors and walls are 20 M.D.C. per 12 ft (3.6 m) unless stated. Belly cargo bay: 20 tons plus 6-8 small vehicles and 12-20 power armor suits, reached by an elevator platform (75 M.D.C.; its belly hatch 100). Cargo: 30x40x20 ft (9.1x12x6.1 m), 250 ton limit. Lights: four light towers, two undercarriage light domes with laser distancers reading out to one mile, a belly light and camera turret storing 5,000 hours of footage, and headlights on the bridge and arms. Robot P.S. 40 for the large arms, 30 for the small retractable ones, which reach out another 7 ft (2.1 m) fitted with lamp, camera, plasma torch and laser cutter (4 ft/1.2 m reach; 5D6 S.D.C., 1D6x10 S.D.C., 1 M.D., 1D4 M.D. or 2D4 M.D. settings); the large arms carry the same kit. Power: nuclear, 12 year average life. Hand to hand at half the attacks and half the Heavy Ground Robot Training bonuses (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352): large arms 4D6 M.D. punch, tear or pry, 2D6 M.D. crush, power punch 6D6+6 M.D.; small arms 1D6 M.D., power punch 2D6 M.D.; stomp or trample 2D6+4 M.D.; body block 3D6 M.D. with a 01-80% chance to knock down a foe of its size or smaller. Sensors beyond the NG standard: thermal imager, infrared and ultraviolet optics, infrared searchlights (500 ft/152 m), enhanced radar (96 targets, 100 miles/160 km), enhanced sonar (96 targets, 50 miles/80 km), a language translator, passive nightvision (2,000 ft/610 m), many cameras (2 mile/3.2 km telescopic), and a scrambled audio/video relay radio of 500 miles (800 km).', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.107-112'),
  ('ng-v50-bigfoot', 'Bigfoot Combat Robot', 'rifts', 'robot', 'Three: pilot, an outside gunner on the laser turret, and a communications officer', NULL, 'Running 45 mph (72 km); leaping not possible', 'Not possible', 'Cannot swim; walks the bottom at up to 20 mph (32 km); maximum depth 2,000 ft (610 m), which the exposed gunner survives only in environmental armor or by magic', 'Height 26 ft 5 in (8 m); width 16 ft (4.9 m) at the upper torso, 11 ft (3.3 m) at the hips and 20-25 ft (6.1-7.6 m) across the feet in most stances; length 32 ft (9.7 m) from back to gun muzzles', '24', 450, 1, NULL, 49000000, '49 million credits. Fair availability.', 'Model Type: NG-V50 (headed NG-V50 Bigfoot). Class: Infantry Assault Robot. A heavier walking gun platform inspired by the Gunbot, in the Hunter Mobile Gun line: no arms, and the whole upper body is a turret turning 360 degrees with a 30 degree vertical arc. The pilot sits between the two plasma cannon barrels; the laser gunner rides an open turret above; the communications officer sits in a separate suite on the right side that also holds backup controls (driven from there, speed drops 30%, the robot is -1 attack per round and the cannons fire only one at a time). Built as a battlefield relay: its radar, sonar and radio data reach friendly units in range and can be bounced between relays for hundreds of miles. Swivel hips and huge feet halve its chance of being knocked down. Slow; often posted behind cover, and used to guard NG hovertrains. Robot P.S. 35. Cargo: one small locker, room enough for a rifle and pistol, a survival kit, some personal belongings and 10 gallons (37.9 liters) of drinking water. Power: nuclear only, 12 year average life; the weapons draw too much for fuel cells or batteries. Hand to hand per Heavy Ground Robot Training (Rifts Ultimate Edition p.352), but no punches, power punches or leap kicks, and stomps only work on targets under 5 ft (1.5 m). Sensors beyond the NG standard: enhanced radar tracking 120 targets at 100 miles (160 km) with a threat-ranking IFF database that gives the Bigfoot and every allied robot, tank and power armor within one mile (1.6 km) receiving its feed +1 on initiative and +1 to strike; enhanced sonar (96 targets, 50 miles/80 km); a scrambled audio/video relay radio of 500 miles (800 km); a shotgun microphone (400 ft/122 m); a tactical camera holding 48 hours; a language translator; thermal imager; infrared and ultraviolet optics; passive nightvision (2,000 ft/610 m); and three smoke grenade launchers on the suite.', 'Rifts World Book 33: Northern Gun One p.112-116');

